   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Small endings, big meanings",
     "texto": [
      "**Diminutives.** The main one is **-ito / -ita**: casa → **casita**; libro → **librito**; poco → **poquito**. Words ending in **-e**, **-n** or **-r**, and short words, take **-cito** or **-ecito**: café → **cafecito**; joven → **jovencito**; pueblo → **pueblecito**; flor → **florecita**.\n\nSpelling follows the sound: po**c**o → po**qu**ito; ami**g**o → ami**gu**ito.\n\nA diminutive rarely means only «small». It adds affection (**abuelita**), softens a request (**¿Me esperas un momentito?**), or makes something sound unimportant (**un problemilla**). Other endings: **-illo** (chico → **chiquillo**) and **-ín** (pequeño → **pequeñín**). Names take them too, and so do adverbs: **Carlitos**, **Anita**; **ahorita**, **despacito**, **tempranito**.\n\n**Augmentatives.**\n\n- **-ón / -ona**: big, or doing something a lot. **Un sillón** was once a big silla. **Un comilón** eats a lot; **un dormilón** sleeps a lot.\n- **-azo**: big and impressive (**un cochazo**, **un golazo**), or a blow given with something (**un portazo**, **un golpazo**, **un codazo**).\n- **-ote**: big and a bit clumsy (**grandote**, **un librote**).\n\nCareful: **-ón** changes the gender of some words: **la** silla, **el** sillón; **la** película, **el** peliculón.\n\n**Contempt.** **-ucho** and **-ucha**: **una casucha**, **feúcho**, **un cuartucho**.\n\n**Intensifying prefixes.** Colloquial: **super-** (superbién, superguapo) and **requete-** (requetebién). More formal: **archi-** (archiconocido) and **ultra-**.\n\n**Words that are no longer diminutives.** Some have become independent: **la mesilla** (bedside table), **el bolsillo** (pocket), **la ventanilla** (window of a ticket office), **el cigarrillo**, **el pañuelo**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "What each ending adds",
     "texto": [
      "Choose the ending according to what you want to say, not only the size.\n\n- **Affection or courtesy**: **-ito, -ita, -cito**. A grandmother says **un cafecito**; a waiter, **un momentito**.\n- **Big size**: **-ón, -ona, -ote**. **Un sillón**, **un librote**.\n- **Blow or admiration**: **-azo**. **Un portazo** is a blow; **un cochazo** is an impressive car.\n- **Contempt**: **-ucho, -ucha, -aco**. **Una casucha**. It can offend, so use it only with people you trust.\n- **Intensity**: **super-, requete-, archi-**. **Requetebién** is colloquial; **archiconocido** is a little more formal.\n\nWatch the **gender**: **-ón** can turn a feminine word masculine (la película → el peliculón). And not every diminutive keeps its meaning: **la mesilla** is already a piece of furniture."
     ],
